A||||8||JohnnyK||Battery up front.||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||11-25-2000||04:48 PM||||I am thinking of making that steel box to hold my battery up front, but am wondering about the actual connection. Where would i buy that long of battery cable, how much would it cost, and where would I connect it to at the engine? Just connect the cables right to where it used to connect to the battery? I'm lost.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000001||strdream||11-25-2000||06:35 PM||||Just out of curiousity, why would a lost guy want to move the battery up front.<P>First off make sure you make good connections<BR>Ground your engine to the unibody.  Neg. Bat post to unibody.  Run one good cable like a #2 from the pos. post to the starter, the post you take the pos. cable off from.<P>You can buy the stuff in bulk a real parts store.  Cable, ends, ties and advise etc.  <P>Ross||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||11-25-2000||06:44 PM||||Boating supply stores also a good stock of cable and fittings||||reg||1
Z||000003||the need for speed||11-25-2000||07:21 PM||||welding cable is what chris west uses.pretty cheap to,||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||11-26-2000||12:32 AM||||That's what I used too, 2 gauge welding wire that is sold by the foot. I bought mine in a farm and ranch supply store. The positive cable connects to the big terminal on the starter. The ground can be connected almost anywhere to the space frame.||||reg||1
Z||000005||William Federle||11-26-2000||08:08 AM||||You can get 25 feet of any color #2 battery cable from JC Whitney for $29.00. I checked welding supply places in Fond du Lac as well as auto supply places. They either didn't have it or wanted twice as much.<P>The hard part is finding a crimper big enough to crimp the terminals on. Autozone has the terminals. I work at a machine tool company and they let me take home a crimper for #2 wire overnight.<p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 11-26-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||11-26-2000||12:11 PM||||You can also solder the ends on. It's a little tricky with wire that big but it meens you'll never get corrosion inside the end.<P>To make it easier to service, you might want to consider normal pre fab cables for as much of this as you can. The ground cables should be fine with this. If you use a really short prefab cable for the + terminal and connect it to a terminal block.... This gives you factory grade ends at the battery. <P>Don't forget you also have to extend the thin wire that comes off the battery. That wire runs the whole car and charges the battery. except 84... 84 uses connections of both the thin wire and the starter.<P>I'd use 10-12ga wire with 105C insulation for that. You can get that at most electrical supply places. maybe home depot or lowes or another large store like those.<P>The 105C is the max working temperature of the insulation. Building codes require it for applications that will be hot. It's allot tougher than normal wire insulation.<P>another thing you can do to prevent corrosion of crimp on terminals is work dielectric into the wire and terminal before crimping. before you bolt it to the frame coat everything with some RTV silicone. Yes, the cleaned off frame too. doing that will seal out water just like when you do it to any other conector on the car.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 11-26-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||JohnnyK||11-26-2000||03:23 PM||||The positive connects to the starter? Thats it? What if I just run a long cable and hook it up to where the positive used to connect to the battery?||||reg||1
Z||000008||RNGCRSH||11-26-2000||03:31 PM||||You need to make the conection at the starter. The one thing you don't want is a splice in your batery cable. If you have ever seen a battery detonate from a chafed wire short you would know what I mean. just run 2 long cables from front to back (+) to starter  and (-) to the original ground point. If you don't have A/C then you can run them inside of some pvc pipe in that channel in the frame. also dont forget to run the Accesory lead also 10 or 12 guage wire?.||||reg||1
